/* Generated by scripts/build-areas.py: researched places with no published coordinates. */
window.ATLAS_UNPLACED_MORE = [{"name": "Rabta chemical plant (Pharma 150)", "when": "", "text": "Reported chemical weapons plant at Rabta, south of Tripoli, publicly presented by Libya as a pharmaceutical factory; subject of the 1990 fire dispute. GlobalSecurity's compilation places the site at Qabilat az Zaribah (Rabta), about 60 miles south of Tripoli. It gives construction as 1984 to 1988. West Germany's intelligence service (BND) and government linked German firm Imhausen Chemie to the project from 1984 onward, and in 1987 the BND judged from satellite images that the plant was most likely for chemical weapons. The US assessed Rabta in 1990 as the largest chemical weapons plant in the developing world; Libya denied this and claimed a closure that was not confirmed. In March 1990 a fire occurred: Libya blamed the United States, while US officials said satellite imagery showed only minimal damage and called it a hoax; US officials later said production resumed by mid-May 1990. In 1995 Libya reopened it as a pharmaceutical plant, a joint venture with Egypt's Nasr Companies. Libya joined the Chemical Weapons Convention in early 2004. The OPCW stated that Libya declared several chemical weapons production facilities which were destroyed or converted to peaceful uses; the OPCW pages seen do not name Rabta, so its exact post-2004 use is not confirmed here. Location approximate: no coordinates were seen on any page opened, so lat/lon are null. Distance is converted from a source figure of about 60 miles (about 97 km) and is rough; other accounts give different distances. Much of the 1980s-90s detail is Western intelligence or press assertion, denied by Libya.", "src": {"t": "GlobalSecurity: Rabta", "u": "https://www.globalsecurity.org/wmd/world/libya/rabta.htm"}}, {"name": "Ruwagha chemical weapons depot", "when": "", "text": "Remote desert depot in south-eastern Libya where Libya's declared mustard stockpile was stored and destroyed under OPCW monitoring (2010-2014). Libya joined the Chemical Weapons Convention in January 2004 and declared nearly 25 tonnes of bulk mustard agent and thousands of unfilled aerial bombs. The OPCW describes the depot at Ruwagha as being in remote south-eastern Libya, far outside the Tripoli area. Waddan is the nearest town named in the brief. Empty munitions were destroyed in March 2004. Destruction of the bulk mustard began in October 2010 and stopped in February 2011 after equipment failed and unrest began. OPCW inspectors confirmed the stockpile intact in November 2011. Work resumed on 20 April 2013 and ended 4 May 2013; the OPCW announced it on 6 May 2013. Libya completed destruction of its remaining mustard-loaded shells and bombs on 26 January 2014. Precursor chemicals (846 tonnes) remained and were later moved to Germany, completed in 2016-2017 per Wikipedia; the OPCW pages seen cover only the earlier steps. The site's state today is not documented in the pages opened. No coordinates seen, so lat/lon null; far from Tripoli (distance not stated in the sources). Wikipedia also reports rebels finding a mustard stockpile in Jufra District in 2011; whether this is the same depot as Ruwagha is not established here.", "src": {"t": "OPCW: Libya completes destruction of its Category 1 chemical weapons", "u": "https://www.opcw.org/news/article/libya-completes-destruction-of-its-category-1-chemical-weapons"}}, {"name": "Rixos Al Nasr Hotel and Convention Centre", "when": "", "text": "A hotel and conference complex that housed foreign journalists in 2011, was a loyalist stronghold during the fall of Tripoli, and later became the seat of the General National Congress. On 4 March 2011 foreign journalists, about 130 invited by the government, were barred from leaving the hotel without a minder. By 22 August, as the rebels entered Tripoli, loyalists still held it. Roughly 40 foreigners, mostly journalists, were kept inside, and Al Jazeera said they were being used as human shields. Saif al-Islam Gaddafi appeared at the hotel in the early hours of 23 August. The journalists were released in Red Cross vans around 24 August. The Timeline of Tripoli says the General National Congress began meeting in the 'Ghabat Al Nasr Convention Centre' in 2012. The High Council of State occupied the former GNC headquarters at the Rixos Al Nasr Convention Centre on 22 April 2016, and GNC loyalists attacked it on 10 October 2016. It was seized again in October 2016, besieged in March 2017, and a Reuters report dated 24 May 2019 says rockets hit the Rixos hotel. No coordinates were found in any source seen. The sources use two names (Ghabat Al Nasr and Rixos Al Nasr) and it is not confirmed that they are the same building.", "src": {"t": "CS Monitor: Spots to watch in the battle for Tripoli", "u": "https://csmonitor.com/World/Middle-East/2011/0822/Spots-to-watch-in-the-battle-for-Tripoli/Rixos-Hotel"}}, {"name": "Undeclared nuclear-programme sites (Al Hashan, Al-Khalla, Salah Eddin)", "when": "", "text": "Group of sites near Tripoli that Libya disclosed in 2003-04 as hosting its undeclared nuclear programme; later cleared by US, UK and IAEA teams. NTI's IAEA-based summary names Al-Hashan (operated 1997-2002), Al-Khalla (its successor), Salah Eddin, Janzour, Sawani, Al-Karamia and El-Ezeizia as undeclared sites, plus underground storage at Sabha. Wikipedia says that in October 2000 a single complete device was installed at Al Hashan. Exact addresses are not published in the pages seen. Libya's 19 December 2003 announcement ended its nuclear weapons efforts. The IAEA verified the sites in 2004, and NTI reports that the US and UK worked with the IAEA to remove all nuclear-related equipment and materials in three stages, with the most sensitive items first and the work done by September 2004. The IAEA then reported on the programme to its Board. The pages seen do not describe the present state of these buildings. Equipment was removed in 2004, so no weapons-related material is reported to remain, but the sites' current use is not documented here. Location approximate: no coordinates were seen, so lat/lon are null; the sources give functions, not locations. Several are described only as being in the Tripoli area. This profile groups sites and should not be pinned to one spot.", "src": {"t": "NTI: Libya nuclear facilities", "u": "https://www.nti.org/analysis/articles/libya-nuclear-facilities/"}}, {"name": "1 September 1969 coup in Tripoli (radio station and barracks)", "when": "", "text": "The overnight coup that ended the monarchy; in Tripoli, officers seized the radio station, barracks, anti-aircraft batteries and airports. On 1 September 1969 Free Officers under Gaddafi staged a coup while King Idris was in Turkey for treatment, launching in Benghazi and finishing within two hours. In Tripoli, Khweldi Hameidi captured the radio station, Umar Muhayshi took over the barracks, and Abdessalam Jalloud seized the anti-aircraft batteries. The officers also occupied airports, police depots and government offices. Hameidi was sent to arrest Crown Prince Hasan ar-Rida and make him renounce his claim. Military control of Tripoli was established within a few days. Gaddafi ended the monarchy and proclaimed a republic. The queen's private secretary was placed under house probation by officers led by Omar Almheishi and later released after negotiations, by her own account. Idris went into exile in Egypt and was sentenced to death in absentia in 1971. The pages opened give no addresses for the radio station or barracks, and the Wheelus connection is not documented. A memoir by the queen's secretary is a personal account. The 'police depots' are named without saying which force held them; no source opened says they were Military Police, and the Military Police Command compound is documented only from the 1980s (see its own profile). No coordinates are given.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: 1969 Libyan revolution", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1969_Libyan_revolution"}}, {"name": "Piazza Italia / Independence Square / Green Square / Martyrs' Square", "when": "1930s expansion", "text": "The central square beside the Red Castle that has changed names with each regime. The Italians built it on the site of the old bread market (souq al-khubs) and expanded it several times in the 1930s; Florestano Di Fausto designed the square around the castle. After independence in 1951 it was called Independence Square. Streets meeting there include Omar Mukhtar Avenue, Independence Street (leading to King Idris's palace), 24 December Avenue and Mizran Street. A large fountain designed by an Italian architect stood at its centre, and a seafront avenue is marked by two tall pillars. After 1969 it was renamed Green Square after the Green Book. The Royal Miramare Theatre, opposite the Red Castle, was demolished by his government after the 1960s to make room for mass rallies. Protests took place there on 20 and 25 March 2011. Rebels took it on 21-22 August 2011 and renamed it Martyrs' Square; crowds celebrated there on 31 August and 2 September. No coordinates were seen on any page opened. It lies directly in front of the Red Castle entrance, but no exact centre is given.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Martyrs' Square (Green Square), Tripoli", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_Square,_Tripoli"}}, {"name": "Libyan state TV headquarters (Al-Jamahiriya TV)", "when": "", "text": "The Tripoli headquarters of Libya's state broadcaster, which carried Gaddafi-era government coverage and was struck by NATO in 2011. Station building work in Tripoli and Benghazi began in 1967 and finished by the end of 1968; regular television started on 24 December 1968. Under Gaddafi it was renamed from the People's Revolutionary Broadcasting Corporation to the Libyan Jamahiriya Broadcasting Corporation on 24 December 2001. The main channel, Al-Jamahiriya, covered People's Congress sessions and readings of the Green Book. It reached the Arab world and Europe by satellite from 1997. On 30 July 2011 NATO struck the state TV's Tripoli headquarters and two installations; the broadcaster said three journalists died and 21 were hurt. Loyalists took refuge at the TV centre on 20 August 2011, and the channels went off air by 21-22 August as rebels took the building. The corporation was dissolved. A relaunched channel later broadcast from Cairo. The exact street address of the 2011 headquarters is not given in the sources seen; no coordinates. Casualty figures come from the broadcaster and were unverified.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Libyan Jamahiriya Broadcasting Corporation", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Libyan_Jamahiriya_Broadcasting_Corporation"}}, {"name": "Al-Fatah missile project", "when": "", "text": "Libya's domestic ballistic missile development effort of the 1990s, publicly reported but with no confirmed location in the sources used. Reports summarised by GlobalSecurity and Wikipedia say Libya sought to build a domestic missile (reported range 950-1,000 km), drawing on earlier OTRAG work in the desert near Sebha from 1979. Foreign help from Iraq, Iran, Serbia and China was alleged. A test facility is mentioned but not located. Per GlobalSecurity, 1995 reports said Iraqi technicians were working on Al Fatah, and in 1999 US imagery satellites reportedly photographed an enlarged Al Fatah test facility. Reports of North Korean talks later proved false after 2003. In December 2003 Libya agreed to eliminate missiles beyond 300 km range and 500 kg payload. Libya largely abandoned the programme in 2004 in exchange for sanctions relief, per Wikipedia. No site is known to remain; nothing in the sources opened describes its present state. No coordinates: the facility's location is not given in any source I opened, so lat/lon are null; location approximate. Much of the programme is reporting by intelligence-sourced press, and its real progress is unclear.", "src": {"t": "GlobalSecurity: Libyan Missiles", "u": "https://www.globalsecurity.org/wmd/world/libya/missile.htm"}}, {"name": "Sabha yellowcake store and 2011 chemical stock reports", "when": "", "text": "Post-2011 concerns about Libyan stockpiles at Sabha (uranium ore concentrate) and chemical stocks reported in the Jufra area; far from Tripoli. NTI lists underground yellowcake storage at Sabha as one of the undeclared sites disclosed in 2004. The IAEA visited it and in December 2011 recommended a quick sale and transfer, warning that storage conditions were worsening. In 2013, the UN said 6,400 barrels were stored near Sabha. In September-November 2011 rebels and press reported chemical stocks near Jufra, and Libya told the OPCW on 1 November 2011 that more suspected stocks had been found. An OPCW team confirmed the declared mustard stockpile at Ruwagha intact in November 2011. On 9 December 2013 the UN's Tarek Mitri said an IAEA team would verify the Sabha stockpile. The pages seen do not give the present state. The 2013 account says the barrels remained under a Libyan army battalion at a former military installation near Sabha. No coordinates (Sabha is in the far south, well outside the 100 km range; distance not stated in the sources, so null). I did not find a source seen for the Misrata or 2011 Rabta claims in the brief, so they are not covered. Jufra reports are press claims.", "src": {"t": "Ahram/AFP, 9 Dec 2013: UN atomic agency to inspect Libyan yellowcake stockpile", "u": "https://english.ahram.org.eg/News/88705.aspx"}}, {"name": "Libyan Scud / short-range missile units", "when": "", "text": "Libya's Soviet- and North Korean-supplied short-range missiles; unit and storage locations near Tripoli, Sirte, Sebha and Al-Jufra are not published in the sources used. GlobalSecurity says Libya obtained Scud-B and FROG-7 missiles from the Soviet Union in the mid-1970s. GlobalSecurity also refers to North Korean Scud-C missiles. OpenStreetMap lists an 'Artillery and Missile School' near Tripoli at 32.766 N, 13.197 E, though no source ties it to Scuds. Two Scuds were fired at Lampedusa in April 1986 after the US raid. After the 19 December 2003 deal, Libya agreed to remove missiles over 300 km range, so it gave up Scud-C but not 300 km Scud-B, per GlobalSecurity. In 2011 Libyan forces reportedly fired Scud-B at rebel areas and missed; NATO strikes reportedly destroyed the rest. Not documented in the pages seen; some missiles are believed to have survived outside government control after 2011 but no source opened confirms this. No base coordinates; distances not given. Claims about Scud storage near Sirte, Sebha, Al-Jufra are repeated in general press but were not confirmed in sources I opened.", "src": {"t": "GlobalSecurity: Libyan Missiles", "u": "https://www.globalsecurity.org/wmd/world/libya/missile.htm"}}, {"name": "Tripoli air-defence network (1986)", "when": "", "text": "Libya's surface-to-air missile network around Tripoli, suppressed in the 1986 US raid; individual sites are not publicly located in the sources used. The account of the 1986 raid cited on Wikipedia says Libyan defences in the whole country included four S-200 (SA-5) units and 86 S-75/S-125 (SA-2/SA-3) units, with Tripoli alone also holding SA-6, SA-8 and Crotale units. Individual battery locations around Tripoli are not given. In April 1986 US A-7E and F/A-18A aircraft fired anti-radar missiles at air defence networks in Tripoli and Benghazi while EF-111s and EA-6Bs jammed radars. The account says no SAM launches homed on US aircraft, and one SA-6 tracked an A-6, which evaded it. Libya claimed five major radars destroyed; this is a Libyan claim. Libya's air defences were again targeted by NATO in 2011. The pages seen do not state which sites survive, whether any are reused, or what their condition is today. No coordinates or distance: location approximate and not published for individual batteries. The 'SA-5 near Tripoli' positions are widely repeated but I did not find a source with locations; treat as unverified.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: 1986 United States bombing of Libya", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1986_United_States_bombing_of_Libya"}}, {"name": "University of Libya, Tripoli faculties (1957)", "when": "1957", "text": "The Tripoli branch of the first Libyan university, which began with a Faculty of Science. The University of Libya began in 1957 and its Tripoli branch started with the Faculty of Science, one of the first practical colleges in the country. The Faculty of Agriculture was added in 1966. After the 1969 coup the Tripoli and Benghazi campuses were subject to a crackdown. A college of petroleum and minerals was added in 1972. In 1973 the University of Libya split into Tripoli and Benghazi universities, and in 1976 Tripoli was renamed Al Fateh University after student unrest that April. Political activity was heavily restricted from the early 1970s; by the late 1970s opponents could be arrested. The medical school was ordered to admit about 2,000 students against a capacity of roughly 150. Renamed University of Tripoli in 2012. The present main campus is in south-east Tripoli at about 32.856 N, 13.221 E. The original 1957 site of the Faculty of Science is not stated, so no coordinates are given. The campus coordinates (32°51'21\"N 13°13'17\"E) are the later university, not necessarily the 1957 building.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: University of Tripoli", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Tripoli"}}, {"name": "Dar Bishi Synagogue", "when": "", "text": "A synagogue in the old Jewish Quarter that was the focus of a short-lived reopening in 2011. Dar Bishi stood in the Hara Kebira, the old Jewish Quarter of the medina. Tripoli had about 14,000 Jews in 1903, and in 1941 Jews were about a quarter of the city, with 44 synagogues. Rioters in November 1945 looted nearly all the city's synagogues and destroyed five. Italy's racial laws from 1938 removed Jews from public office and state schools. Most Jews left after 1948 and 1967. After 1969 Jewish property was confiscated and fewer than 40 Jews remained by 1972. Diarna says the last member of the Jewish community died in 2003 (another source says the last Jew was allowed to leave that year). In 2011 David Gerbi began clearing the building and reopened it for prayer, but work stopped on 8 October 2011 after protests. Condition and ownership unknown; Diarna made a 3D digital reconstruction. Foundation date and its exact street are not given in sources opened. Wikipedia says Gerbi removed a brick wall blocking the entrance. Later reports of conversion or demolition were not verified, so no coordinates are given.", "src": {"t": "Diarna: Dar Bishi synagogue", "u": "https://diarna.org/?p=65"}}, {"name": "Banco di Roma and Banco di Napoli buildings", "when": "1907 (Banco di Roma); 1913 or 1930 (Banco di Napoli)", "text": "Branches of the first Italian banks in Tripoli, plus the Bank of Italy headquarters of the 1920s. Banco di Roma was the first Italian bank in Tripoli, opening in 1907. Banco di Napoli received authority for a Tripoli branch in December 1911, and its Tripoli building stood on Corso Vittorio Emanuele; its sources date the opening to 1913 or to an inauguration on 30 November 1930. The Bank of Italy building went up in the 1920s under governor De Bono. The Banco di Roma building is also marked on a tourist map of the old city, and directions to the Othman Pasha madrasa say to walk toward the Banco di Roma from Bab al-Bahr. On 13 November 1969 the Libyan government nationalised Banco di Napoli and turned it into a Libyan joint-stock company. Its Tripoli operations had resumed fully in 1952 after British oversight began in 1946. The Italian community was expelled in 1970. Exact addresses and architects are not given in the sources opened. Which present building is the Banco di Roma is unconfirmed, so no coordinates. Banco di Napoli operations resumed in 1952 after British oversight in 1946.", "src": {"t": "Intesa Sanpaolo International History: Libya", "u": "https://internationalhistory.intesasanpaolo.com/world-map/countries/detail/IT-ISP-MAPPAESTERA-0000123/libya"}}, {"name": "Libya's 2003 disarmament deal and 2004 removals", "when": "", "text": "Libya's December 2003 decision to end its weapons programmes and the 2004 US-UK-IAEA removals from sites around Tripoli. On 19 December 2003 Gaddafi announced Libya would voluntarily end its nuclear, chemical and long-range missile efforts. US and UK inspection teams had visited sites in October and December 2003. This is a national event, so no single location is mapped. The IAEA, with US and UK help, removed nuclear-related equipment and materials in three stages, completing the work in September 2004 (per NTI). Highly enriched uranium fuel left Tajoura for Russia in March 2004. Libya joined the Chemical Weapons Convention in early 2004 and made its first declaration; sanctions relief followed. Libya's weapons programmes were declared dismantled. After 2011 the sites were left lightly guarded; the OPCW found the Ruwagha stockpile intact in November 2011. No location; the exact number of visited sites varies by account (GlobalSecurity cites 10 in one account and dozens in others). Dates on the Chemical Weapons Convention entry differ between 6 January and 5 February 2004 in Wikipedia.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Libya and weapons of mass destruction", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Libya_and_weapons_of_mass_destruction"}}, {"name": "Italian colonist villages around Tripoli", "when": "1938", "text": "Planned agricultural settlements founded for Italian farming families, some of them near Tripoli. Italian colonists concentrated in the farmland around Tripoli. In 1938 Italy founded 26 agricultural villages for about 20,000 colonists, including Gioda, Crispi, Olivetti and Breviglieri. Settlers had to come from large families, pass medical and background checks, and have a household head in the Fascist party. The Tripoli area held the largest Italian community in the colony: 47,400 Italians among 113,212 residents in 1939. In January 1939 the coastal areas became part of metropolitan Italy. In 1970 Gaddafi ordered the expulsion of about 20,000 remaining Italians and nationalised Italian companies. Their property was confiscated, valued at about 3 billion euros today. The Italian population had fallen to about 1,500 by 1986. The exact locations of the villages near Tripoli were not given in sources opened, so no coordinates. Wikipedia says most of the farming villages were in Cyrenaica. The internment camps of the 1930s were in Cyrenaica, not Tripoli, so none are listed here.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Italian settlers in Libya", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_settlers_in_Libya"}}, {"name": "Lampedusa missile attack launch site (April 1986)", "when": "", "text": "Libya's missile reprisal on 15 April 1986 against the US Coast Guard LORAN station on Lampedusa, Italy; launch site often said to be near Sirte but not confirmed. The reprisal followed Operation El Dorado Canyon. Wikipedia says Libya fired two Scud missiles at the NATO base at Capo Ponente, Lampedusa, at about 16:33 UTC on 15 April 1986. One exploded about 2 km northwest and the other about 2 km southwest of the base, in the sea. Libya later said it targeted a US-led NATO base, not Italy. The attack caused a diplomatic crisis with Italy. The page seen does not state the launch site; reports that it was near Sirte, east of Tripoli, were not confirmed in the sources I opened. Lampedusa is unchanged and is today an Italian island; the launch location remains unrecorded in the sources used here, and no site is mapped. Launch site not confirmed by any page I opened; 'Sirte' is common in press but unverified here. Lat/lon null.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Libyan missile attack against Lampedusa", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Libyan_missile_attack_against_Lampedusa"}}, {"name": "Royal Miramare Theatre", "when": "1925-1928", "text": "The largest theatre in Tripoli, a 2,000-seat Italian opera house opposite the Red Castle, now demolished. Opened in 1925 as the Real Teatro Miramare during the governorship of Emilio De Bono; Wikipedia's timeline lists it with 1928 buildings. It seated 2,000 and staged operas, including Verdi's Un ballo in maschera, and hosted Italian and European companies. It stood opposite the Corniche, across from the Red Castle. Allied bombing in World War II heavily damaged it, and it was still maintained until independence. Wikipedia says Gaddafi's government demolished it after the 1960s to make room for large demonstrations in what became Green Square. A Libyan blog instead says it was demolished in the late 1960s, before the coup. Gone. A 2018 lecture on its history was held at Dar Hassan al-Faqih in Tripoli. Demolition date (late 1960s vs after 1969) and architect are not settled. No coordinates are given.", "src": {"t": "Libyan Wanderer: Tripoli's lost elegance of Miramare Theatre", "u": "https://libyanwanderer.com/tripolis-lost-elegance-of-miramare-theatre/"}}, {"name": "Hara Kebira (Jewish Quarter)", "when": "", "text": "The historic Jewish quarter inside the Old City, where Dar Bishi and many other synagogues stood. Hara Kebira (\"great quarter\") was the main Jewish neighbourhood of the Old City. Jews ran many shops in nearby Souq al-Turk and Souq al-Hrara in the Italian period. The Alliance Israélite counted about 14,000 Jews in Tripoli in 1903; in 1941 they were 25 percent of the population with 44 synagogues. The 1945 riots (5-7 November) left about 4,000 Jews homeless. Violence in June 1948 killed 12 more, and after the 1967 war over 6,000 were evacuated to Rome. After the 1969 coup Jewish property was confiscated and debts owed to Jews were cancelled. Jewish men were arrested, and fewer than 40 Jews remained by 1972. One account says the last Jew, an elderly woman, was allowed to leave in 2003. Exact boundaries of the quarter were not documented in the sources opened. Its present occupants and condition are unknown.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: History of the Jews in Libya", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_Jews_in_Libya"}}, {"name": "Grand Hotel of Tripoli (Italian original)", "when": "1920s", "text": "A large Italian-era seafront hotel of 350 rooms near the cathedral, later demolished and replaced. Designed by Florestano Di Fausto in 1920 according to a Libyan blog, though Wikipedia's timeline lists the Grand Hotel under 1925 and says it may date from 1924. It stood in an open park overlooking the port near what is now the Algerian Embassy and the Gamal Abdel Nasser (former cathedral) Mosque. The hotel had 350 rooms and was known for its many arches. It sits next to the Al Waddan Hotel. The blog says the original was demolished in the 1980s and a new hotel on the same site was designed by a British company in 1982. The exterior wall of the new building shows a picture of the original. The new hotel was deserted after 2011 according to the blog. Design year (1920) and opening year (1924/1925) differ; the demolition date and the 1982 rebuilding come from one blog citing a Facebook group. Corinthia Hotel Tripoli (2003) is a different building. No coordinates found.", "src": {"t": "Libyan Wanderer: The Grand Hotel of Tripoli", "u": "https://libyanwanderer.com/the-grand-hotel-of-tripoli-another-lost-architectural-beauty/"}}, {"name": "Dar Bishi Synagogue", "when": "", "text": "The last surviving synagogue in the Old City, designed in 1922 by Umberto Di Segni and now derelict. The old city once had about 33 synagogues; Dar Bishi is the only one still standing. In the 1980s the Libyan government tried to restore it but abandoned the effort. Tripoli's last indigenous Jewish community member died in 2003. In 2011 Libyan Jewish exile David Gerbi returned, cleared debris and reopened it for prayer, but on 8 October 2011 protests forced the work onto indefinite hold and he was urged to leave. The building is in very poor shape, with refuse on the floors and most windows bricked up; some parts have been demolished. Reports suggest local authorities planned to turn it into an Islamic library. No coordinates found. It lies in the Hara Kebira (old Jewish quarter) of the Old City. The Islamic library plan is a report, not confirmed.", "src": {"t": "Diarna: Dar Bishi", "u": "https://diarna.org/?p=65"}}, {"name": "Tripoli International Fair", "when": "1927", "text": "The oldest trade fair in Africa, founded by the Italian colonial government. Founded by the Italian government in 1927 and promoted alongside the Tripoli Grand Prix to showcase Italian Libya. In the 1930s it was one of the main international fairs of the colonial world; the 1934 fair became known for a first air trip across an oasis of the Libyan Sahara, commemorated by an Italian stamp. The most notable structure is a symbolic monument at the entrance, the Pavilion of the Governorate of Rome, by the Egyptian-born Italian architect Alessandro Limongelli. After World War II the fair reopened on a much smaller scale. The 2008 edition ran 2-12 April and in 2010 over 1,000 participants from 29 countries took part. The fairground location, its Gaddafi-era role, and whether the pavilion survives were not found, so no coordinates.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Tripoli International Fair", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripoli_International_Fair"}}, {"name": "Jamahiriya institutions: People's Committees and Revolutionary Committees", "when": "", "text": "The state system of Gaddafi's Jamahiriya, run from Tripoli through committees and congresses rather than ministries. People's Committees began around 1973 in the cultural revolution, organised by function (universities, firms, media) and geography, with over 2,000 by mid-1973. The General People's Congress (1977) replaced the Revolutionary Command Council, and the General People's Committee replaced the cabinet, with 'secretaries' instead of ministers. Revolutionary Committees, organised from 1977, supervised the system, surveyed the population, repressed opposition and ran public executions; in 1979 they took control of Basic People's Congress elections. The system ended with the Jamahiriya in October 2011. This is a system, not a single building; no committee office locations were found.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia - Libyan Arab Jamahiriya", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Libyan_Arab_Jamahiriya"}}, {"name": "People's Hall (General People's Congress)", "when": "", "text": "The large hall where the General People's Congress met under Gaddafi. Protesters set it on fire in February 2011 and it later burned repeatedly. Built in the late 1970s or early 1980s, it hosted the General People's Congress and was normally closed to the public. Demonstrators set it on fire on 21 February 2011 and it was reignited on 22 February and burned out, along with other government buildings including the Justice Ministry at Martyrs' Square. In March 2016 militia commander Haithem Tajouri's brigade took over the hall; it was later abandoned, used by gangs, and set on fire several times, most recently on 13 April 2017. The Libyan Heritage House lists it as destroyed following the 2011 revolution. No coordinates were found; exact location is not given on the pages seen. Current condition is not documented.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: People's Hall, Tripoli", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People%27s_Hall,_Tripoli"}}, {"name": "Central Bank of Libya headquarters", "when": "", "text": "The headquarters in Tripoli of Libya's central bank, which began as the National Bank of Libya in 1956. The bank was set up by a 1955 law and began operating on 1 April 1956 as the National Bank of Libya. It became the Bank of Libya in 1963 and took its present name after the 1969 coup. The sources seen do not describe the building itself or events at it between 1969 and 2011. The bank's administration split during the 2011 civil war and again from 2014, and it announced reunification in August 2023. In August 2024 the Tripoli government removed governor Sadiq al-Kabir, prompting the east-based government to close oil fields; Naji Belqasem has been governor since October 2024. The building's address and any damage in conflicts are not documented in the sources seen; no coordinates.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Central Bank of Libya", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Bank_of_Libya"}}, {"name": "Lungomare Conte Volpi (the Corniche)", "when": "1924", "text": "The Italian-built waterfront boulevard along the harbour, laid out under governor Giuseppe Volpi. Giuseppe Volpi governed Tripolitania from 1921 to 1925 and hired architect Armando Brasini to lead its renovation, including the waterfront boulevard. Wikipedia's timeline dates the Lungomare Conte Volpi to 1924. A Columbia University lantern-slide record shows it as \"Il Lungomare Conte Volpi\" in the early 20th century. The promenade was once considered among the most beautiful on the Mediterranean. In the 1970s a road expansion project filled in a long stretch of the sea, so the Corniche has no sea in front of it; the Red Castle also lost its sea frontage and Saraya Lake formed. The extent of the original waterfront is not mapped here, so no point is given. Brasini's role is stated in a blog.", "src": {"t": "Libyan Wanderer: Tripoli's lost elegance of Miramare Theatre", "u": "https://libyanwanderer.com/tripolis-lost-elegance-of-miramare-theatre/"}}, {"name": "Police college (Tripoli, location not published)", "when": "", "text": "A police training college whose cadets graduated in Tripoli; a Human Rights Watch report also lists a Police Academy among Tripoli sites used in a Muslim Brotherhood retrial. Libyan state news (JANA) reported on 4 September 1985, during the 16th anniversary celebrations of the 1969 revolution, a ceremony for the graduation of the 10th batch of the police college. The director spoke of training cadets to be ideologically committed to the revolution's goals, and the secretary of the General People's Committee for Justice attended. Human Rights Watch (2006) lists the Police Academy among Tripoli sites in connection with a retrial of Muslim Brotherhood defendants. None of the sources opened gives an address, so it is not mapped. It is not shown to be the same as the 1969 'police depots' or the Military Police Command. The 2006 HRW detail comes from a summary of its report, not a page read in full here.", "src": {"t": "CIA reading room (via archive.org): JANA, 4 Sep 1985, graduation ceremony for police cadets", "u": "https://archive.org/details/cia-readingroom-document-cia-rdp05-01559r000400400057-9"}}, {"name": "Tripoli Central Station and the Tripoli-Tajura line", "when": "1910s-1920s", "text": "The Italian-built station that began three narrow-gauge lines, including a 21 km line east to Tajura. Tripoli Central was the start of three Italian lines: Tripoli-Zuara (118 km), Tripoli-Garian (90 km) and Tripoli-Tagiura (21 km, ten stations). They used 950 mm gauge, and Gurgi and Gargaresc were the first two stops on the Zuara and Garian lines. Italy built nearly 400 km of railway in Libya and none after 1926. After the 1911 Ain Zara battle the Italians built a line linking that oasis to Tripoli. A Tripoli-Benghazi line begun in 1941 was never finished. Wikipedia lists Tripoli Central station among the Italian-era buildings later demolished, without a date. Opening years for each line, the tramway named in the request, and the station site are not documented in the sources opened; no coordinates are given.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Italian Libya Railways", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_Libya_Railways"}}, {"name": "Karamanli Mosque (Ahmed Pasha Karamanli Mosque)", "when": "", "text": "An 18th-century mosque complex founded by Ahmed Pasha Karamanli, founder of the Karamanli dynasty, with a madrasa and family tombs. A source places it in the Souq al-Mushir area. Ahmed Karamanli (1686-1745) seized power in Tripoli in the 1711 coup and made the governorship hereditary. Construction of the mosque began in 1736 and the complex includes a madrasa and the tombs of dynasty members. The sources seen say nothing about the mosque in 1969-2011. The mosque is listed as active. It was vandalised in 2014 during the civil conflict, damaging its ceramic tiles and marble decoration, and UNESCO condemned the attack. Whether repairs were made is not stated. No coordinates were found. The sources seen do not say whether Ahmed Pasha's own tomb is in the complex.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Karamanli Mosque", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karamanli_Mosque"}}, {"name": "Ahmed Pasha Karamanli Mosque", "when": "1736-1738", "text": "The mosque, madrasa and dynastic mausoleum founded by Ahmed Karamanli, near the clock tower and Souq al-Mushir. Ahmed Karamanli, founder of the dynasty that ruled from 1711, began the mosque in 1736. Another source gives 1736-1738. It has 25 domes, a madrasa and a mausoleum for members of the family. It stands beside Souq al-Mushir, next to the Red Castle, and a 1994 photograph shows it framed with the clock tower through a souq gate. The Karamanlis ruled Tripolitania until the Ottomans removed them in 1835. In 2014, during fighting in Libya, the mosque was vandalised and its ceramic tiles and marble decoration were damaged; UNESCO condemned the attack. No coordinates were seen on any page opened. Completion date and any Italian or Gaddafi-era restoration were not found.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Karamanli Mosque", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karamanli_Mosque"}}, {"name": "1945 anti-Jewish riots in Tripoli (sites)", "when": "", "text": "The 5-7 November 1945 riots under British military administration, in which synagogues and Jewish homes in Tripoli were attacked. The riots ran from 5 to 7 November 1945 in Tripolitania, then under British occupation, after a wave of rioting on 2 November in Aleppo and Cairo. In Tripoli, 38 people were killed; the toll across the region was over 140, including 36 children. Rioters looted nearly all of Tripoli's synagogues and destroyed five, and plundered over 1,000 Jewish homes and businesses. About 4,000 Jews were left homeless. The British were criticised for responding slowly; at least 550 Muslims were arrested and tried in British military courts. Which streets and synagogues in Tripoli were hit is not named in the sources opened, so no coordinates are given. The page's town totals add to 135 not \"more than 140\". Villages such as Amrus (40 killed), Zanzur (34), Tajura (7) and Zawia (13) were also hit. Further riots in June 1948 killed 12.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: 1945 Tripoli pogrom", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1945_Tripoli_pogrom"}}, {"name": "1967 Six-Day War riots in Tripoli", "when": "", "text": "The June 1967 anti-Jewish and anti-Western riots that triggered the departure of most of the remaining Jewish community. On 15 June 1967 Libyan youths held violent demonstrations backing the Arab states at war with Israel, which led to the deaths of many Jews and the destruction of US and British property. Wikipedia says the unrest included attacks on US and British embassies and oil company offices. After the war, anti-Jewish violence killed 18. King Idris allowed Jews to leave temporarily, and the Italian Navy helped evacuate over 6,000 of them to Rome within a month. About 7,000 remaining Jews were evacuated overall, according to another page. The pages opened do not say which of these attacks were in Tripoli, or name particular buildings. Casualty figures differ. No coordinates are given.", "src": {"t": "Libyan Heritage House: Turbulent time, 1960s", "u": "https://libyanheritagehouse.org/a-turbulent-time-closure-of-wheelus-pan-arabism/project-one-f5w4d-w2bym"}}, {"name": "Bab al-Bahr (Sea Gate)", "when": "", "text": "The harbour-side gate of the Old City, giving its name to the quarter around the Dragut and Gurgi mosques. Bab al-Bahr opens from the old city toward the harbour. Dragut, killed at Malta in 1565, was buried in the nearby Sidi Darghut Mosque, close to this gate. The Gurgi Mosque (1834) is in the Bab al-Bahr district near the sea, and the Othman Pasha madrasa (1654) lies on the central axis running from the seafront into the city. The date of the gate itself, and any Italian-era changes, were not found in the sources opened. Visitors reach the medina from here; its present state is not documented in the sources opened. No coordinates were seen on a page I opened, and the gate does not appear in the OpenStreetMap extract, so none are given. Gate date is unknown.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Tripoli, Libya", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripoli,_Libya"}}, {"name": "Souq al-Turk", "when": "Ottoman period", "text": "One of the longest markets of the Old City, a covered and open street that became a showcase for fine textiles and jewellery. Created by an Ottoman governor and named for its resemblance to open markets in Turkey. It lies in two connected parts, one covered opposite the clock tower and one open. Under the Italians its character shifted from oriental to western European, and it was crowded on Sundays. Traders were mostly Jewish, with some Italians, Indians and Africans. Jewish families ran toy and sewing shops there. Nearby Souq al-Hrara sold silk and was also mostly Jewish-run. Which governor founded it and when is not clear; the source names a \"Wali Mohamed Bahsa\" with a doubtful spelling. Souq al-Rabaa was not found in sources opened. No coordinates.", "src": {"t": "Libyan Wanderer: Tripoli's old-city markets", "u": "https://libyanwanderer.com/?p=1029"}}, {"name": "Central Bank of Libya", "when": "", "text": "Libya's central bank, in the former Savings Bank building on Al Fatah Street, designed in 1921 by Armando Brasini. Founded 1955 as the National Bank of Libya and opened 1 April 1956; renamed Bank of Libya in 1963 and Central Bank of Libya after the 1969 coup. In March 2011 governor Farhat Bengdara resigned and joined the rebels, first arranging for most external assets to be frozen. The bank split in the civil wars; the Tripoli and Bayda branches agreed to unify in 2021 and announced reunification in August 2023. Naji Belqasem has been governor since 3 October 2024. No coordinates found.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia - Central Bank of Libya", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Bank_of_Libya"}}, {"name": "Hammam Dragut (Darghouth Turkish Bath)", "when": "1604 or 1670", "text": "An Ottoman bath attached to the Dragut Mosque and tomb in the Old City. The bath is annexed to the Sidi Darghut Mosque and tomb. Wikipedia dates it to 1081 AH (1670-71). Its timeline separately lists an Iskandar Pasha hammam built in 1604, and the mosque page says Iskander Pasha built or renovated a bath in the early 1600s. The old-city map from a travel site names three hammams: Hammam Darghut, Hammam al-Kebira and Hammam Alheygha. A Manchester research record also covers the surviving historic hammams of the medina. Present use is not stated in the sources opened. Whether the 1604 Iskandar Pasha bath and the 1670-71 Darghouth bath are the same building is unclear. No coordinates were found.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Darghouth Turkish Bath", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darghouth_Turkish_Bath"}}, {"name": "Fort Hamidiye and Fort Sultaniye (1911 landing)", "when": "before 1911", "text": "The two Ottoman harbour forts shelled by the Italian fleet on 3-4 October 1911. Starting at 15:30 on 3 October 1911 an Italian squadron fired on Forts Hamidiye and Sultaniye from about 9,500 m, then closer, leaving the forts badly damaged; the Ottoman gunboat Seyyad and the steamer Derna were scuttled. Captain Umberto Cagni's naval landing force went ashore on 5 October, took Fort Sultaniye and raised the Italian flag at noon, while sappers took Fort Hamidiye. By 16:30 troops reached the marketplace. Italian strength in the city rose to about 35,000 by 11 October. Neither fort was matched to a surviving building or to an OpenStreetMap feature; no coordinates are given. The Wikipedia battle page shows the city centre only.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Battle of Tripoli (1911)", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Tripoli_(1911)"}}, {"name": "Bab Jedid (New Gate) and Bab Zanata", "when": "1860-65", "text": "A western gate of the Old City opened in the 1860s to replace the earlier Bab Zanata. Bab Zanata was blocked during the civil war of 1831 under Ali II Karamanli. An inscription dated 12 February 1865 says residents asked the Ottoman governor to open a new gate in its place, and it became known as Bab Jedid, the New Gate. Wikipedia's timeline dates the opening of Bab el-Jedid to 1860, so sources differ on the year. The gate sits on the western side of the old city. One of the main gates of the medina according to the page opened; present condition not documented. Year (1860 vs 1865) differs between sources. It is not stated whether Bab al-Hurriya (Freedom Gate) is the same as Bab Jedid. No coordinates were found.", "src": {"t": "Alive in Libya: Al-Madina gates and alleys", "u": "https://alive-in.org/unveiling-al-madinas-gates-and-alleys-a-photographic-journey-through-the-tripolis-historical-old-city-in-libya"}}, {"name": "Uthman Pasha Madrasa", "when": "1654", "text": "A 17th-century Quranic school beside the Dragut Mosque that still teaches. Founded in 1654 under Ottoman rule and named for the governor Othman Pasha al-Saqizli. It lies in the Bab al-Bahr area near the Dragut entrance, on the axis leading from the sea into the city. A square courtyard leads to more than ten marble rooms under two Ottoman domes, and a courtyard shows inscriptions and Ottoman taps. Mohammed Kamel Ben Mustafa, born in 1828 in Zawiya, studied and taught there. Still operating as a religious school with study circles, according to a 2020s article. Whether Othman Pasha personally paid for it is not stated. No coordinates were found.", "src": {"t": "Libyan Wanderer: Othman Pasha School in Tripoli", "u": "https://libyanwanderer.com/othman-pasha-school-in-tripoli/"}}, {"name": "Tripoli International Fair", "when": "", "text": "A trade fair ground on Omar Mukhtar Avenue, founded by the Italians in 1927 and described as the oldest trade fair in Africa. Founded in 1927 by the Italian government and used in the 1930s to promote Italian Libya; its entrance monument, the Pavilion of Rome, was designed by Alessandro Limongelli. It reopened at reduced scale after the Second World War. The 2008 session ran 2-12 April; in 2010 more than 1,000 participants from 29 countries took part, organised by the General Board of Fairs. The sources seen do not describe the site after 2010. No coordinates were found. Events in 2011 and the present state are not documented in the sources seen.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Tripoli International Fair", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripoli_International_Fair"}}, {"name": "Souq al-Mushir", "when": "1906-1908; rebuilt 1935", "text": "An open-air market street named for the Ottoman governor Rajab Pasha, running from the Huwara souq toward the clock tower. Built by the Ottoman governor Rajab Pasha, who ruled Tripoli in 1906-1908, and named for his title al-Mushir. It runs from the opening of Souq al-Huwara on the south-east toward the clock tower. It originally sold flowers, medicinal herbs and gardening supplies. The Karamanli Mosque stands on it. A timeline gives 1935 for the souq's reconstruction under the Italians. Now sells clothes, household goods, food and hardware. The Italian reconstruction of 1935 is listed only in one timeline. OpenStreetMap maps a gate called Bab al-Mushir near the Red Castle, but the souq itself has no coordinates here.", "src": {"t": "Libyan Wanderer: Tripoli's old-city markets", "u": "https://libyanwanderer.com/?p=1029"}}, {"name": "Old City gates (Bab al-Bahr, Bab Zanata, Bab Hawwara, Bab el-Jedid)", "when": "", "text": "The historic gates of the walled Old City: Bab al-Bahr on the sea side, Bab Hawwara to the south-east, Bab Zenata to the west, and the later Bab el-Jedid. Wikipedia names Bab al-Bahr (north, near the sea), Bab Hawwara (south-east, probably today's Bab al-Mensha) and Bab Zenata (west, originally Bab al-Ashdar). The Timeline of Tripoli says Bab el-Jedid opened in 1860. Only sections of the walls remain. The sources seen give no events at the gates between 1969 and 2011. Only sections of the city walls survive. Individual gate positions were not found, so no coordinates are given. Bab Darghouth has its own entry.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Tripoli, Libya", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripoli,_Libya"}}, {"name": "Sidi Salem Mosque", "when": "late 15th century; restored 1670", "text": "An old mosque of the medina that predates the Spanish conquest and was restored in the Ottoman period. Wikipedia dates Sidi Salem to the late 15th century with a restoration in 1670. That places it before the Spanish conquest of 1510, the Knights' rule from 1530 and the Ottoman capture of 1551. Its timeline repeats the 1670 restoration. It is one of the older religious buildings of the old city alongside the Naqa Mosque. No founder or architectural description was found in the pages opened. Location within the medina, founder and later history are not documented in the sources opened. No coordinates are given.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Tripoli, Libya", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripoli,_Libya"}}, {"name": "Libyan Jamahiriya Broadcasting Corporation / Al-Jamahiriya TV", "when": "", "text": "The state broadcaster of the Jamahiriya, strictly controlled by the state, with its headquarters in Tripoli. NATO struck the Tripoli headquarters of Al-Jamahiriya and two of its installations on 30 July 2011; the station said three journalists were killed and 21 wounded, which was not independently verified. NATO said the aim was to stop 'terror broadcasts'. Al-Jamahiriya, Al Urubah and Al-Shababiyah went off air in late August 2011. Later organisation not researched. No coordinates found for the headquarters; the radio buildings were not documented.", "src": {"t": "RSF - NATO attacks on national TV headquarters", "u": "https://rsf.org/en/news/nato-attacks-national-tv-headquarters-and-installations-tripoli"}}, {"name": "Souq al-Mushir", "when": "", "text": "An open-air market in the Old City named for the Ottoman governor Rajab Pasha, running between the Huwara souq and the clock tower. A local source says it was built under al-Wali al-Mushir Rajab Pasha between 1906 and 1908; the Timeline of Tripoli says it was reconstructed in 1935. It includes the Karamanli mosque and sells clothing, household goods, food and hardware today. The sources seen give nothing for 1969-2011. Still an open-air market. No coordinates found; the two sources disagree on dates.", "src": {"t": "Libyan Wanderer: Souq al-Mushir", "u": "https://libyanwanderer.com/?p=1029"}}, {"name": "Faculty of Medicine, University of Tripoli", "when": "", "text": "Tripoli's medical school, opened in 1973 and located next to the university's main campus. Libya's medical schools opened in Benghazi (1970) and Tripoli (1973) on a British-style curriculum. The faculty has its own administration although it was planned as part of the main university, and trains students at Tripoli Medical Center, Tripoli Central Hospital, Al-Khadra and Al-Jalaa hospitals. Still an active faculty of the University of Tripoli. No coordinates were found for the faculty building itself.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia - University of Tripoli", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Tripoli"}}, {"name": "Libyan Open University", "when": "", "text": "A distance-learning university based in Tripoli, established in 1987. History: Established in 1987 as an education foundation affiliated with the Ministry of Higher Education, it began scientific activity in the 1989/1990 academic year. Its mission was to spread distance learning without limits of place, time or age. It lists itself as a member of the Arab Universities Union and the Arab Network for Open Education Learning. No street address or coordinates found; only a Tripoli P.O. box was listed. Naser University and Libyan International Medical University could not be sourced (the latter is generally a Benghazi institution).", "src": {"t": "Libia Open University listing", "u": "https://datainforms.faraafrica.org/node/242376"}}, {"name": "Tripoli Congress Center", "when": "", "text": "A conference building of about 25,000 square metres designed by TA Tabanlioglu Architects and listed as built in 2009-2010. The architects list the client as the State of Libya, with a two-storey rectangular block wrapped in a metal mesh shield and set among woodland. Its listed date is 2009-2010. The sources seen do not state how it was used before 2011. Its present use is not described in the sources seen. No coordinates found. It is not confirmed whether this is the building owners call the Palace of Conferences or Dhafra Convention Centre.", "src": {"t": "TA Tabanlioglu: Tripoli Congress Center", "u": "https://www.tabanlioglu.com/project/tripoli-congress-center/"}}, {"name": "Ahmed Pasha Karamanli Mosque", "when": "", "text": "An 18th-century mosque complex in Tripoli's old city founded by Ahmed Karamanli, with a madrasa and tombs of the dynasty. History: Construction began in 1736 (inscriptions suggest 1737-38). No Gaddafi-era events were found. It was vandalised in 2014 during the civil war: ceramic tiles and marble decoration were damaged, and UNESCO condemned the attack. It is listed as active. No coordinates found.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia - Karamanli Mosque", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karamanli_Mosque"}}, {"name": "Royal Miramare Theatre (demolished)", "when": "", "text": "An Italian-era theatre across from the Red Castle, demolished under Gaddafi's government to create space for mass demonstrations. Built in 1928, it stood opposite the Red Castle on Martyrs' (then Green) Square. Gaddafi's government demolished it after the 1960s to make room for large demonstrations in the square. The theatre no longer exists. Exact demolition date and exact site are not given; no coordinates.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Martyrs' Square, Tripoli", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martyrs%27_Square,_Tripoli"}}, {"name": "Darghouth Turkish Bath (Hammam Darghut)", "when": "", "text": "A Turkish bath in the Old City founded around 1670 and attached to the Sidi Darghut mosque and tomb. The bath was established in 1081 AH (about 1670-1671), annexed to the Sidi Darghut mosque and tomb. The sources seen give no information on its use during the Gaddafi period. Its present state is not described in the sources seen. No coordinates found; present use and Gaddafi-era history are undocumented in the sources seen.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Darghouth Turkish Bath", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darghouth_Turkish_Bath"}}, {"name": "Ministry of Justice (Martyrs' Square)", "when": "", "text": "A justice ministry building at Martyrs' Square that was burned in the February 2011 protests. The People's Hall article says demonstrators set fire to several buildings in February 2011, including the justice ministry at Martyrs' Square. The sources seen give no earlier history. Not described in the sources seen. The OSM extract shows a separate 'Ministry of Justice' point elsewhere in Tripoli, so exact building is unconfirmed; no coordinates given.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: People's Hall, Tripoli", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People%27s_Hall,_Tripoli"}}, {"name": "National Oil Corporation headquarters", "when": "", "text": "The headquarters of Libya's National Oil Corporation in central Tripoli. The sources seen give no history before 2011. On 10 September 2018 armed men stormed the building, a blast followed, and a fire spread on lower floors; at least two staff and two gunmen died and ten were wounded. No coordinates found; Gaddafi-era use is not documented in the sources seen.", "src": {"t": "Al Jazeera: NOC Tripoli offices attacked", "u": "https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/news/2018/9/10/libya-national-oil-corporations-tripoli-offices-attacked"}}, {"name": "GMR Stadium (Al-Madina SC ground)", "when": "", "text": "An 8,000-capacity football ground in Tripoli, home of Al-Madina SC. History: A timeline of Tripoli dates its opening to 2000. Al-Madina SC was founded on 29 October 1953 and is the only Libyan club never relegated. Al-Madina plays in the Libyan Premier League western group in 2025-26. No coordinates found. No sources were found for an Al-Ahli Tripoli club ground other than the national stadium, or for an indoor basketball arena.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia - Al-Madina SC", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Madina_SC"}}, {"name": "Tripoli Central Railway Station (demolished)", "when": "", "text": "The Italian-built central railway station of Tripoli, which was demolished under Gaddafi. Built under Italian rule, the station was demolished under Gaddafi, according to Wikipedia's article on Tripoli. The sources seen give no date or location. No station remains. Date of demolition and site are not given in the sources seen.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Tripoli, Libya", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripoli,_Libya"}}, {"name": "Uthman Pasha Madrasa", "when": "", "text": "An Ottoman madrasa built in 1654 and one of the best-preserved in Tripoli, with a courtyard, student rooms and a domed tomb chamber. Wikipedia's article on Tripoli says it was built in 1654. The sources seen give no events for 1969-2011. Not described. No coordinates, no recent history.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia: Tripoli, Libya", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tripoli,_Libya"}}, {"name": "Tripoli Fish Market", "when": "", "text": "A busy seafood market at the edge of the Old City. The sources seen do not cover its history. A travel site describes a busy market selling sardines, mackerel, sea bream, shrimp and other seafood, with a cleaning area. No coordinates, history or 2011 information found.", "src": {"t": "Travel2Unlimited: Tripoli Fish Market", "u": "https://travel2unlimited.com/libya-tripoli-fish-market/"}}, {"name": "Libyan Studies Centre", "when": "", "text": "A research and historical documentation centre in Tripoli that opened in 1978. History: A timeline of Tripoli records that the Libyan Studies Center opened in 1978. Only the opening date was found; the Arabic name and the location are not confirmed here. The National Library was not found in the sources.", "src": {"t": "Wikipedia - Timeline of Tripoli", "u": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_Tripoli,_Libya"}}];
